  "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{\u2032}(-3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-5,-2].$ Notice that $x=-3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947756/assets/59159512fa1b51477f171584a3717e2bf2774e8c940b59525fe478e1bcf6aece.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x}=\\frac{1-4}{-2-(-5)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(-3)=-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
